    a: "Colonic diverticular disease is a disease of older adults, not young infants, and this age misattribution is what makes the statement false. It develops over years as chronic constipation and a low-fibre diet raise intraluminal pressure against a colonic wall that weakens with age at the points where blood vessels penetrate it. The other listed features — a wall-weakness mechanism, a constipation-related cause, and a silent course until inflammation develops — are all genuinely true of the disease.",
